WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.073
- [Julia] Thank you to
Brilliant for supporting PBS.

00:00:04.560 --> 00:00:05.430
- Hey, hun?

00:00:05.430 --> 00:00:06.840
- Yeah?

00:00:06.840 --> 00:00:09.963
- What would you do
with a billion dollars?

00:00:12.290 --> 00:00:15.761
I think I'd spend one
third on fighting poverty.

00:00:15.761 --> 00:00:18.520
One third towards medical research,

00:00:18.520 --> 00:00:20.020
and one third on reversing climate change.

00:00:26.027 --> 00:00:28.713
- [Spaceship Operator] T minus five, four,

00:00:28.713 --> 00:00:31.913
three, two, one, lift off.

00:00:35.021 --> 00:00:36.438
- [Phillip] Woo!

00:00:37.473 --> 00:00:38.478
- Philip?

00:00:38.478 --> 00:00:40.083
- Yeah, what you said.

00:00:41.411 --> 00:00:43.994
(upbeat music)

00:00:49.820 --> 00:00:53.450
What does it mean to be a billionaire?

00:00:53.450 --> 00:00:56.140
It's not someone who
makes a billion a year

00:00:56.140 --> 00:00:59.010
or has a billion in cash
stored away somewhere,

00:00:59.010 --> 00:01:00.800
like some people believe.

00:01:00.800 --> 00:01:02.650
Instead, a billionaire is someone

00:01:02.650 --> 00:01:06.275
whose net worth is at or above $1 billion.

00:01:06.275 --> 00:01:09.580
Meaning, after subtracting
their debts and liabilities,

00:01:09.580 --> 00:01:12.480
their assets add up to a billion or more.

00:01:12.480 --> 00:01:15.235
Assets can include cash,
real estate, stocks,

00:01:15.235 --> 00:01:18.100
and pretty much anything
that is valuable enough

00:01:18.100 --> 00:01:19.520
to trade in for money.

00:01:19.520 --> 00:01:24.520
- As of 2021, there are 2,755
billionaires in the world.

00:01:25.060 --> 00:01:27.948
Which is 660, more than last year.

00:01:27.948 --> 00:01:31.530
Their net worth can fluctuate,
but as of early November,

00:01:31.530 --> 00:01:36.530
Elon Musk was in the top spot
with a staggering 307 billion.

00:01:36.960 --> 00:01:39.040
He recently eclipsed the current runner-up

00:01:39.040 --> 00:01:40.940
Amazon founder, Jeff Bezos.

00:01:40.940 --> 00:01:44.090
Over the last year, these
men have made money so fast

00:01:44.090 --> 00:01:47.780
that if they saw $1,000
lying on the ground,

00:01:47.780 --> 00:01:51.530
it literally would not be
worth their time to bend down

00:01:51.530 --> 00:01:52.490
and pick it up.

00:01:52.490 --> 00:01:55.230
- Billionaires have
more money than anyone

00:01:55.230 --> 00:01:57.380
could reasonably spend in a lifetime,

00:01:57.380 --> 00:02:01.930
leading many to wonder is
it evil to be that rich?

00:02:01.930 --> 00:02:03.870
This is a complicated question

00:02:03.870 --> 00:02:05.770
that could cover several episodes.

00:02:05.770 --> 00:02:09.500
And because it's about ethics,
it's fairly subjective.

00:02:09.500 --> 00:02:12.215
But we'll look at some of the
main arguments in this debate,

00:02:12.215 --> 00:02:14.980
give our thoughts and then let you decide.

00:02:14.980 --> 00:02:17.510
- First, let's look at the wealth gap

00:02:17.510 --> 00:02:21.080
between the richest and
poorest people in America.

00:02:21.080 --> 00:02:26.080
Economic inequality more than
doubled between 1989 and 2016.

00:02:26.420 --> 00:02:29.690
Jeff Bezos, Bill Gates and Warren Buffett,

00:02:29.690 --> 00:02:33.990
collectively own more
wealth than the bottom half

00:02:33.990 --> 00:02:36.330
of all American households.

00:02:36.330 --> 00:02:38.920
That's more wealth held by three people

00:02:38.920 --> 00:02:42.130
than by over 160 million.

00:02:42.130 --> 00:02:44.920
During the first year of
the COVID-19 pandemic,

00:02:44.920 --> 00:02:48.350
billionaires grew 54% richer

00:02:48.350 --> 00:02:51.270
while the number of people
in poverty around the world

00:02:51.270 --> 00:02:53.840
increased by over a hundred million.

00:02:53.840 --> 00:02:57.310
- Some defenders of billionaires
argue that they deserve

00:02:57.310 --> 00:03:00.670
their wealth because
of all add to society,

00:03:00.670 --> 00:03:02.140
like job creation.

00:03:02.140 --> 00:03:04.280
For instance, Amazon currently has

00:03:04.280 --> 00:03:08.207
about 1.3 million
employees around the globe.

00:03:08.207 --> 00:03:10.470
But are those jobs really new

00:03:10.470 --> 00:03:13.463
or just shifted from the
thousands of small retailers

00:03:13.463 --> 00:03:15.204
that have gone out of business

00:03:15.204 --> 00:03:17.720
during Amazon's rise to dominance?

00:03:17.720 --> 00:03:21.240
- While there's no way to
assign an exact amount of blame

00:03:21.240 --> 00:03:22.916
for other companies' demise,

00:03:22.916 --> 00:03:25.439
there is no doubt that giant retailers

00:03:25.439 --> 00:03:28.860
like Amazon and Walmart
steer customers away

00:03:28.860 --> 00:03:30.380
from smaller companies.

00:03:30.380 --> 00:03:32.770
And whether you're a
billionaire business owner

00:03:32.770 --> 00:03:34.990
or a billionaire hedge fund manager,

00:03:34.990 --> 00:03:37.680
the easiest way to make
money is to ensure that

00:03:37.680 --> 00:03:40.303
you are hiring as few people as possible

00:03:40.303 --> 00:03:43.420
at wages that are as low as possible.

00:03:43.420 --> 00:03:45.570
- According to the small
business administration,

00:03:45.570 --> 00:03:48.840
firms with less than 500
employees are responsible

00:03:48.840 --> 00:03:51.233
for creating almost half of new jobs.

00:03:51.233 --> 00:03:54.280
So, even though giant
companies undoubtedly

00:03:54.280 --> 00:03:58.590
add jobs to the economy, they
are by no means the only ones.

00:03:58.590 --> 00:04:01.610
- Okay, so what about
innovation and convenience?

00:04:01.610 --> 00:04:04.660
It's hard to deny that
Amazon has made shopping

00:04:04.660 --> 00:04:06.520
a lot quicker and easier.

00:04:06.520 --> 00:04:10.700
And if they're $386
billion in sales last year

00:04:10.700 --> 00:04:11.930
is any indication,

00:04:11.930 --> 00:04:15.740
the service Amazon
provides is quite popular.

00:04:15.740 --> 00:04:18.820
Jeff Bezos certainly deserves
some credit for that,

00:04:18.820 --> 00:04:21.059
but does he deserve all of it?

00:04:21.059 --> 00:04:24.580
- After all, it's not like
he came up with the idea.

00:04:24.580 --> 00:04:27.900
People have been predicting
the advent of online shopping

00:04:27.900 --> 00:04:29.040
for decades.

00:04:29.040 --> 00:04:32.440
The technology that finally
made it possible, the internet,

00:04:32.440 --> 00:04:34.956
was originally developed
with taxpayer dollars.

00:04:34.956 --> 00:04:37.880
And the physical infrastructure depends on

00:04:37.880 --> 00:04:42.480
roads, bridges, electrical
grids, also paid for by taxes,

00:04:42.480 --> 00:04:44.280
AKA you and me.

00:04:44.280 --> 00:04:46.471
It's safe to say that if
Jeff had never been born,

00:04:46.471 --> 00:04:49.448
we'd all still be buying
our books, batteries

00:04:49.448 --> 00:04:51.720
and bug spray, online.

00:04:51.720 --> 00:04:53.799
- We're not saying he
doesn't deserve to be rich,

00:04:53.799 --> 00:04:56.959
he built a company that a
lot of people use and like,

00:04:56.959 --> 00:05:00.461
which no one would do without
a financial incentive.

00:05:00.461 --> 00:05:04.140
But when you take into
account just how many people

00:05:04.140 --> 00:05:08.200
helped earn those billions,
it seems a bit lopsided.

00:05:08.200 --> 00:05:10.140
- That's why some politicians are pushing

00:05:10.140 --> 00:05:13.492
for a 70% income tax on the ultra wealthy,

00:05:13.492 --> 00:05:16.130
to help close the inequality gap.

00:05:16.130 --> 00:05:17.890
Now, that number might sound high,

00:05:17.890 --> 00:05:20.050
but it's actually much
closer to what it was

00:05:20.050 --> 00:05:23.120
between 1932 and 1981.

00:05:23.120 --> 00:05:27.770
During that time, the top tax
rate never fell below 63%,

00:05:27.770 --> 00:05:31.160
and rose as high as 94%.

00:05:31.160 --> 00:05:34.300
Some are also pushing for
an additional wealth tax

00:05:34.300 --> 00:05:36.680
of up to 6% on billionaires,

00:05:36.680 --> 00:05:40.010
which would tax all
assets in their net worth.

00:05:40.010 --> 00:05:41.580
- Sounds great in theory,

00:05:41.580 --> 00:05:43.810
but there's no guarantee it would work,

00:05:43.810 --> 00:05:47.750
since billionaires are
notorious for dodging taxes.

00:05:47.750 --> 00:05:49.974
The lawyers, consultants and accountants

00:05:49.974 --> 00:05:54.040
that make up the so-called
wealth defense industry,

00:05:54.040 --> 00:05:57.960
can find tax breaks and
loopholes, hide money offshore,

00:05:57.960 --> 00:06:01.160
and employ other tricks
that are technically legal,

00:06:01.160 --> 00:06:03.080
but no doubt sketchy.

00:06:03.080 --> 00:06:06.490
Bringing billionaires to
an income tax bill of,

00:06:06.490 --> 00:06:09.360
well relatively zero.

00:06:09.360 --> 00:06:12.231
- One of the sneakier ways
billionaires avoid taxes

00:06:12.231 --> 00:06:15.180
is by taking out loans.

00:06:15.180 --> 00:06:16.740
Yes, you heard that correctly.

00:06:16.740 --> 00:06:20.052
The ultra wealthy borrow
money, lots of it.

00:06:20.052 --> 00:06:23.195
Not because they need
to like regular people,

00:06:23.195 --> 00:06:26.450
but because it allows them
to access their wealth

00:06:26.450 --> 00:06:28.728
without technically producing income.

00:06:28.728 --> 00:06:30.610
- Here's how it works.

00:06:30.610 --> 00:06:33.570
If a billionaire takes out a huge salary,

00:06:33.570 --> 00:06:37.260
they'll pay a 37% income
tax on a bulk of it.

00:06:37.260 --> 00:06:41.002
Which is why so many of them
pay themselves low salaries.

00:06:41.002 --> 00:06:45.880
For instance, Jeff Bezos's
base salary at Amazon in 2020

00:06:45.880 --> 00:06:48.890
was $81,840.

00:06:48.890 --> 00:06:50.720
His real wealth is in the value

00:06:50.720 --> 00:06:54.789
of all the Amazon stock he owns,
which increases every year.

00:06:54.789 --> 00:06:56.982
But if he ever sells any of that,

00:06:56.982 --> 00:07:00.150
to say buy a boat or a new house,

00:07:00.150 --> 00:07:03.650
he'd have to pay 20% in capital gains tax.

00:07:03.650 --> 00:07:06.700
- [Phillip] Instead, what he
and many other billionaires do,

00:07:06.700 --> 00:07:09.158
is take out low interest loans to finance

00:07:09.158 --> 00:07:11.550
their luxurious lifestyles.

00:07:11.550 --> 00:07:14.243
Loans are not considered income by the IRS

00:07:14.243 --> 00:07:18.730
and they can even write off
the interest as a deduction.

00:07:18.730 --> 00:07:20.020
When that loan comes due,

00:07:20.020 --> 00:07:23.060
they'll take out another
one to pay the first one.

00:07:23.060 --> 00:07:25.590
They can keep this going
basically until they die,

00:07:25.590 --> 00:07:29.410
since who would ever deny
a loan to a billionaire?

00:07:29.410 --> 00:07:31.590
- But let's assume for
the sake of argument

00:07:31.590 --> 00:07:33.480
that there is a billionaire out there

00:07:33.480 --> 00:07:36.729
who's paying their taxes
and not exploiting anyone.

00:07:36.729 --> 00:07:39.750
Are they evil just for
having that much money

00:07:39.750 --> 00:07:42.540
when there's so much
poverty, disease and calamity

00:07:42.540 --> 00:07:43.600
in the world?

00:07:43.600 --> 00:07:46.820
Well, not if they're helping
to do something about it.

00:07:46.820 --> 00:07:48.130
And we can't deny,

00:07:48.130 --> 00:07:50.610
there are some very
charitable billionaires.

00:07:50.610 --> 00:07:54.610
Warren Buffett has given
$42 billion in his lifetime.

00:07:54.610 --> 00:07:57.440
Bill and Melinda Gates
have given 30 billion.

00:07:57.440 --> 00:07:59.790
Some billionaires have even given so much

00:07:59.790 --> 00:08:01.900
that they're not billionaires anymore.

00:08:01.900 --> 00:08:03.290
- But the fact of the matter is,

00:08:03.290 --> 00:08:05.240
for every charitable billionaire,

00:08:05.240 --> 00:08:07.751
there are far more hoarding their money.

00:08:07.751 --> 00:08:11.750
Only 223 billionaires,
less than 10% of them,

00:08:11.750 --> 00:08:13.890
have signed The Giving Pledge,

00:08:13.890 --> 00:08:16.420
a promise to give away a
majority of their wealth

00:08:16.420 --> 00:08:19.080
to charity or philanthropy
during their lifetime

00:08:19.080 --> 00:08:20.620
or in their wills.

00:08:20.620 --> 00:08:22.680
And Jeff Bezos is not one of them.

00:08:22.680 --> 00:08:25.491
Though his ex wife McKenzie
Scott, did sign the pledge,

00:08:25.491 --> 00:08:29.350
and has given away over 5
billion since their divorce,

00:08:29.350 --> 00:08:33.640
leaving her with a paltry 55 billion. Sad.

00:08:33.640 --> 00:08:36.070
- But even if every billionaire gave away

00:08:36.070 --> 00:08:38.080
half their money to charity,

00:08:38.080 --> 00:08:41.700
we're still letting a
handful of unelected people,

00:08:41.700 --> 00:08:45.496
decide which causes are
worthwhile and which aren't.

00:08:45.496 --> 00:08:48.745
Ideally, a representative
democracy like ours

00:08:48.745 --> 00:08:52.160
would use taxes and
elections to give the people

00:08:52.160 --> 00:08:54.435
at least some say in how our excess wealth

00:08:54.435 --> 00:08:55.910
should be allocated.

00:08:55.910 --> 00:08:58.702
- Don't get us wrong, the
issue is not black and white.

00:08:58.702 --> 00:09:01.697
It's not like there's some
specific dollar amount

00:09:01.697 --> 00:09:04.720
where a plucky entrepreneur
suddenly becomes

00:09:04.720 --> 00:09:06.745
a scheming super villain.

00:09:06.745 --> 00:09:08.950
Capitalism isn't perfect,

00:09:08.950 --> 00:09:10.910
but it's a system that allows people

00:09:10.910 --> 00:09:13.461
to reap the benefits of
their risks and hard work,

00:09:13.461 --> 00:09:16.247
and has lifted millions out of poverty

00:09:16.247 --> 00:09:18.250
in the last hundred years.

00:09:18.250 --> 00:09:20.110
- That's why some economists say that

00:09:20.110 --> 00:09:23.550
holding billionaires accountable
is actually a defense

00:09:23.550 --> 00:09:24.970
of capitalism.

00:09:24.970 --> 00:09:27.280
The scale of inequality in America today,

00:09:27.280 --> 00:09:29.015
is virtually unprecedented.

00:09:29.015 --> 00:09:33.070
When so many families are
struggling to make ends meet,

00:09:33.070 --> 00:09:35.450
when so many schools and public programs

00:09:35.450 --> 00:09:36.920
are starved for cash.

00:09:36.920 --> 00:09:38.544
Meanwhile, half the nation's wealth

00:09:38.544 --> 00:09:41.281
is being hoarded by a handful of people.

00:09:41.281 --> 00:09:44.730
Well, that's when citizens
start to feel like

00:09:44.730 --> 00:09:46.721
their economic system has failed them,

00:09:46.721 --> 00:09:50.749
and they either want to fix
it or look for another one.

00:09:50.749 --> 00:09:52.427
And that's our two cents.

